page>1403</fpage>&#x02013;1415.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8682874</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Corbett, S.A., and J.E. Schwarzbauer. <year>1999</year>. Requirements for &#x003b1;(5)&#x003b2;(1) integrin- mediated retraction of fibronectin-fibrin matrices. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>274</volume>:<fpage>20943</fpage>&#x02013;20948.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10409640</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Cukierman, E., R. Pankov, D.R. Stevens, and K.M. Yamada. <year>2001</year>. Taking cell-matrix adhesions to the third dimension. <source>Science.</source> <volume>294</volume>:<fpage>1708</fpage>&#x02013;1712.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11721053</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Davidson, L.A., M. Marsden, R. Keller, and D.W. Desimone. <year>2006</year>. Integrin &#x003b1;5beta1 and fibronectin regulate polarized cell protrusions required for <italic>Xenopus</italic> convergence and extension. <source>Curr. Biol.</source> <volume>16</volume>:<fpage>833</fpage>&#x02013;844.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16682346</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Deves, R., and C.A. Boyd. <year>2000</year>. Surface antigen CD98(4F2): not a single membrane protein, but a family of proteins with multiple functions. <source>J. Membr. Biol.</source> <volume>173</volume>:<fpage>165</fpage>&#x02013;177.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10667913</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Diaz, L.A., Jr., A.W. Friedman, X. He, R.D. Kuick, S.M. Hanash, and D.A. Fox. <year>1997</year>. Monocyte-dependent regulation of T lymphocyte activation through CD98. <source>Int. Immunol.</source> <volume>9</volume>:<fpage>1221</fpage>&#x02013;1231.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9310825</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Erickson, H.P. <year>1994</year>. Reversible unfolding of fibronectin type III and immunoglobulin domains provides the structural basis for stretch and elasticity of titin and fibronectin. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>91</volume>:<fpage>10114</fpage>&#x02013;10118.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7937847</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Erickson, H.P. <year>2002</year>. Stretching fibronectin. <source>J. Muscle Res. Cell Motil.</source> <volume>23</volume>:<fpage>575</fpage>&#x02013;580.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12785106</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Fenczik, C.A., R. Zent, M. Dellos, D.A. Calderwood, J. Satriano, C. Kelly, and M.H. Ginsberg. <year>2001</year>. Distinct domains of CD98hc regulate integrins and amino acid transport. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>276</volume>:<fpage>8746</fpage>&#x02013;8752.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11121428</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">F&#x000e9;ral, C.C., N. Nishiya, C.A. Fenczik, H. Stuhlmann, M. Slepak, and M.H. Ginsberg. <year>2005</year>. CD98hc (SLC3A2) mediates integrin signaling. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>102</volume>:<fpage>355</fpage>&#x02013;360.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15625115</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Francis, S.E., K.L. Goh, K. Hodivala-Dilke, B.L. Bader, M. Stark, D. Davidson, and R.O. Hynes. <year>2002</year>. Central roles of &#x003b1;5beta1 integrin and fibronectin in vascular development in mouse embryos and embryoid bodies. <source>Arterioscler. Thromb. Vasc. Biol.</source> <volume>22</volume>:<fpage>927</fpage>&#x02013;933.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12067900</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">George, E.L., E.N. Georges-Labouesse, R.S. Patel-King, H. Rayburn, and R.O. Hynes. <year>1993</year>. Defects in mesoderm, neural tube and vascular development in mouse embryos lacking fibronectin. <source>Development.</source> <volume>119</volume>:<fpage>1079</fpage>&#x02013;1091.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8306876</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">George, E.L., H.S. Baldwin, and R.O. Hynes. <year>1997</year>. Fibronectins are essential for heart and blood vessel morphogenesis but are dispensable for initial specification of precursor cells. <source>Blood.</source> <volume>90</volume>:<fpage>3073</fpage>&#x02013;3081.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9376588</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Grinnell, F. <year>1984</year>. Fibronectin and wound healing. <source>J. Cell. Biochem.</source> <volume>26</volume>:<fpage>107</fpage>&#x02013;116.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6084665</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Hall, A. <year>1998</year>. Rho GTPases and the actin cytoskeleton. <source>Science.</source> <volume>279</volume>:<fpage>509</fpage>&#x02013;514.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9438836</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Hughes, P.E., B. Oertli, M. Hansen, F.L. Chou, B.M. Willumsen, and M.H. Ginsberg. <year>2002</year>. Suppression of integrin activation by activated Ras or Raf does not correlate with bulk activation of ERK MAP kinase. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>2256</fpage>&#x02013;2265.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12134066</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Hynes, R.O. <year>1994</year>. Genetic analyses of cell-matrix interactions in development. <source>Curr. Opin. Genet. Dev.</source> <volume>4</volume>:<fpage>569</fpage>&#x02013;574.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7524834</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Ingber, D. <year>1991</year>. Integrins as mechanochemical transducers. <source>Curr. Opin. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>3</volume>:<fpage>841</fpage>&#x02013;848.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1931084</pub-i